   "start_page": 144,
   "summary": "Despatch No. 5682 transmits Embassy Report No. 1676, a 32-page survey of conditions in Japan for May 1941, accompanied by monthly lists of telegrams and despatches. The report recounts Ambassador Grew's meetings with Foreign Minister Matsuoka concerning the Tripartite Pact, American aid to Britain, Atlantic convoys, and Japan's southward advance, alongside protests against Japanese aerial attacks on American missions, businesses, and consulates in China. It details military campaigns in Shanxi and Hubei, Ambassador Honda's peace proposals for China, the Franco-Japanese economic convention on Indochina, demands upon the Netherlands East Indies, and domestic events such as the establishment of the Tohokai party and tightened state control of agriculture and trade. Attached are complete lists of telegrams (Nos. 620-755) and despatches (Nos. 5556-5630) sent by the Embassy throughout May 1941.",
   "title": "Despatch No. 5682: Report of Conditions in Japan During the Month of May 1941",
   "to": "Cordell Hull, Secretary of State"
  },
